# How this course was made (W3C PROV-O): written by AI agents, then checked
# in the rounds below, against the sources below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://archive.org/details/Fsi-KoreanBasicCourse-StudentText> ,
              <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://en.wiktionary.org/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "The course was planned and written by AI agents working for Anton Wiklund, from a concept for all ten chapters (issue #132), and checked by AI agents and by machines (the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators, its course and Markdown checks, and a second SHACL validator); no person has reviewed it."@en ,
                 "Selection: one topic, food and eating together, told as one story with the same people, so that dishes, spice, portions and thanks come back from chapter to chapter. This first version has chapters 0 to 2 of the ten planned: a first meeting at a café, deciding what to eat, and a meal at a small 찌개 restaurant; later versions continue the story with paying, delivery, the market, cooking, a family dinner, a team dinner and a regular place. The people and places are invented. The level is after Hangul and roughly Basic Korean, about TOPIK I. No card front repeats one of Basic Korean or Everyday Korean phrases; the dialogues use some of their phrases, and the theory says where they were learned."@en ,
                 "Structure: 3 chapters of 9 steps in all, three per chapter: the scene, a dialogue with its key phrases explained; how it works, the chapter's grammar point; and another way it goes, a variant dialogue and a note on culture. Each step is read in two chunks and checks two or three questions met in its theory, and each chapter has two review questions, asked only in its final review: 22 step questions and 6 review questions, 28 questions in all. Every question has one correct option (the card's back) and three wrong options (its distractors), each with a note on why it is wrong. The front is Korean in Hangul, in today's standard spelling and spacing and without romanization, in 해요체 for what the learner says, with the staff's honorific lines marked in the notes as lines the learner hears; the back, note and options are in English, Swedish, German, Spanish and French, with du, tu and tú between the two friends and Sie, usted and vous to and from staff. The theory is in English only."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the course is CC0 1.0. Its dialogues, theory, questions, options and notes are the course's own text, written with the help of AI. The FSI course (a work of the United States Government) and Wikidata (CC0 1.0) allow any reuse, and English Wiktionary (CC BY-SA 4.0) was used to check meanings only; no text was taken from any of the three."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Concept: Korean (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The Korean of the course's concept, with two other concepts it was chosen from: cast, speech levels, the planned card fronts of all ten chapters and the sample step and cards, for correctness, naturalness, standard spelling, overlap with Basic Korean and Everyday Korean phrases, and the release's Markdown rules. Done by an AI agent working independently of the compiling agent; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Applied before the chapters were written: the learner's character is called 알렉스, which names no gender, instead of a name Koreans would hear as slang for teacher; the staff's line 따뜻한 걸로 드릴까요, 아이스로 드릴까요? is carded only as its first half; a guest speaks honorific 해요체 rather than 합쇼체 to a friend's parents, with 합쇼체 kept for set lines; and every dialogue line ends in a hard break before its gloss."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Chapters: Korean, answers and course rules (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: Each chapter as written, reviewed by three AI agents working apart: one for the Korean (grammar, spelling and spacing, naturalness, the speech level each speaker would use, and the glosses and grammar in the theory), one for the backs, notes, wrong options and their notes in English, Swedish, German, Spanish and French (meaning, agreement between the languages, and wrong options that are plausible and do not give the answer away by their form), and one for the course's rules, its Markdown and its questions as flip cards studied later out of context; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 37 findings (18, 10 and 9 in the three chapters), all applied; where two findings proposed different wordings, one was chosen. Among them: the rule for -(으)ㄹ까요 after a stem in ㄹ, -시- explained as honoring the person who acts, 씨 limited to adults on an equal footing, a phrase checked by a step but not met in its theory, wrong options and backs reworded in Swedish, German and Spanish, and notes that now name the honorific 드시다."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Course as a whole (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The three chapters read together by an AI agent for the story's continuity, names and speech levels used the same way throughout, facts taught twice, correct options that stand out by their length or form, fronts repeated within the course or from Basic Korean and Everyday Korean phrases, glosses that disagree with the backs, and the counts in the description; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: two jumps in the story's time were removed, a fact about where a restaurant keeps its spoons was corrected, seven backs or wrong options that stood out by their length were reworded, staff speaking to two customers now use the plural in Swedish and Spanish, and one note's quotation marks were made consistent; the 28 fronts are all different and none repeats one of the other Korean decks."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Sources (AI and machine)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The course's key words against the three sources, searched by an AI agent with scripts: 38 words of the fronts in English Wiktionary's Korean entries, the same words and the dishes in Wikidata through the Wikibase API, and the grammar points (counters, -(으)ㄹ까요?, 못, the honorific -시-, the polite speech styles) in the OCR text of the FSI course. Not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No source contradicts a card. Wiktionary has Korean entries for 32 of the 38 words, among them 연하다 (of a liquid: thin, dilute), 동갑 (age-mate), 맛집, 공깃밥 and 드시다 (honorific of 먹다); 년생, 진동벨, 아무거나, 셀프, 인분 and 앞접시 have none, though 생, 아무것 and 접시 do. Wikidata has lexemes for 6 of the words (고기, 못, 안, 끓다, 분, 씨) and items for 비빔밥, 김치찌개, 찌개, 반찬 and caffè americano. The FSI course has 잔 as the counter for cups and glasses, -(으)ㄹ까요?, 못, -(으)시- and its formal and informal polite speech, which are 합쇼체 and 해요체."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-16T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The whole release, with the library checker (npm run library), which validates it against Solid Memo's shapes (library deck format 6, card format 5, chapter, step and distractor format 1), DCAT-AP and SKOS, with the reference data, checks the course's outline by its rules and its Markdown by the rules for a release, and with a second, independent SHACL validator (pySHACL, npm run crosscheck) over the same shapes."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The release conforms: the library checker found the library valid with this course in it, and pySHACL found all 234 documents it checks conform."@en .

<https://archive.org/details/Fsi-KoreanBasicCourse-StudentText>
    dcterms:title "Korean Basic Course, Volume 1 (Foreign Service Institute, 1968), as scanned on the Internet Archive" ;
    dcterms:creator "B. Nam Park; edited by Augustus A. Koski; Foreign Service Institute, U.S. Department of State" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Checking: the Internet Archive's OCR text of Volume 1 (romanized Korean and English), and Volume 2 in the same item for its English, searched for the course's grammar points and words. Volume 1 explains 잔 as the counter for \"cups or glasses of liquid\" (\"a cup of coffee\"), -(으)ㄹ까요?, 못, the honorific suffix -(으)시- and its formal and informal polite speech, and has 고기 (meat), 맵다 and 김치. A work of the United States Government: the title page reads \"B. Nam Park … Foreign Service Institute, Washington, D.C. 1968\" and \"For sale by the Superintendent of Documents, U.S. Government Printing Office\". 17 U.S.C. § 105: \"Copyright protection under this title is not available for any work of the United States Government\". Section 105 applies in the United States; what the course takes from it is checking of grammar points and meanings, not its text."@en .

<https://www.wikidata.org/>
    dcterms:title "Wikidata (Korean lexemes and items)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikidata contributors (Wikimedia Foundation)" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Checking: Korean lexemes and items queried for the course's key words and dishes on 16 October 2026. Wikidata has few Korean lexemes: 6 of the 38 words have one (고기, 못, 안, 끓다, 분, 씨), and the dishes have items (비빔밥 Q648352, 김치찌개 Q701756, 찌개 Q1062483, 반찬 Q716245, caffè americano Q1152551); 공깃밥 has none. https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing — \"All structured data (i.e. the main, Property, Lexeme, and EntitySchema namespaces) is released into the public domain under Creative Commons Zero.\""@en .

<https://en.wiktionary.org/>
    dcterms:title "English Wiktionary (Korean entries)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wiktionary contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Verification only; no text was taken from it. 38 key words of the fronts were looked up on 16 October 2026: 32 have a Korean entry, and the definitions read agree with the cards (방금 just now, 연하다 of a liquid thin or dilute, 진하다 strong, 동갑 age-mate, 맛집 delicious restaurant, 공깃밥 rice in a bowl, 반찬 side dishes, 드시다 honorific to eat or drink, 맵다 spicy, 끓다 to boil, 잔 a glass of a beverage). 년생, 진동벨, 아무거나, 셀프, 인분 and 앞접시 have no entry. Text is available under CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Wiktionary:Copyrights)."@en .

# Chapter 0: At the café.
<#ch-cafe>
    a solid-memo:Chapter ,
      schema:Syllabus ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    dcterms:title "At the café"@en ,
                  "På kaféet"@sv ,
                  "Im Café"@de ,
                  "En la cafetería"@es ,
                  "Au café"@fr ,
                  "카페에서"@ko ;
    dcterms:description "Alex meets Minji for the first time at a café: finding the person you are meeting, ordering a drink the way you want it, understanding the staff, and asking a peer's age politely. Ends with a review of the chapter."@en ,
                        "Alex träffar Minji för första gången på ett kafé: hitta den man ska träffa, beställa en dryck som man vill ha den, förstå personalen och artigt fråga en jämnårig om åldern. Avslutas med en repetition av kapitlet."@sv ,
                        "Alex trifft Minji zum ersten Mal in einem Café: die Person finden, mit der man verabredet ist, ein Getränk so bestellen, wie man es möchte, das Personal verstehen und höflich nach dem Alter fragen. Endet mit einer Wiederholung des Kapitels."@de ,
                        "Alex conoce a Minji en una cafetería: encontrar a la persona con la que has quedado, pedir una bebida como la quieres, entender al personal y preguntar la edad con cortesía. Termina con un repaso del capítulo."@es ,
                        "Alex rencontre Minji pour la première fois dans un café : trouver la personne qu'on doit retrouver, commander une boisson comme on la veut, comprendre le personnel et demander poliment l'âge. Se termine par une révision du chapitre."@fr ;
    solid-memo:reviewQuestion <#q-cafe-r01> ,
                              <#q-cafe-r02> .

<#ch-cafe-1>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-cafe> ;
    schema:position 0 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Meeting Minji

*Saturday, two in the afternoon. A café near Hongik University station. Alex is meeting a language-exchange partner called Minji for the first time.*

**알렉스:** 저기, 혹시 민지 씨예요?\\
*Excuse me, are you Minji, by any chance?*

**민지:** 네, 맞아요! 알렉스 씨죠? 반가워요.\\
*Yes, that's right! You're Alex, aren't you? Nice to meet you.*

**알렉스:** 반가워요. 많이 기다렸어요?\\
*Nice to meet you too. Have you been waiting long?*

**민지:** 아니요, 저도 방금 왔어요. 먼저 주문할까요?\\
*No, I just got here too. Shall we order first?*

**직원:** 어서 오세요. 주문하시겠어요?\\
*Welcome. Are you ready to order?*

**알렉스:** 아이스 아메리카노 한 잔 주세요. 연하게 해 주세요.\\
*One iced americano, please. Make it weak, please.*

**민지:** 저는 따뜻한 라테 한 잔이요.\\
*A hot latte for me.*

**직원:** 드시고 가세요?\\
*For here?*

**민지:** 네, 마시고 갈게요.\\
*Yes, we'll have it here.*

**직원:** 진동벨이 울리면 가지러 오세요.\\
*Come and pick it up when the buzzer goes off.*

---

**저도 방금 왔어요**: "I just got here too." It is the usual answer to 많이 기다렸어요?, even when it is not quite true.

**연하게 해 주세요**: "Make it weak, please." 연하다 means weak or mild; the opposite is 진하다 (strong), so 진하게 해 주세요. -게 해 주세요 asks for something to be made a certain way, like 안 맵게 해 주세요, which you know from Everyday Korean phrases.

**혹시** means "by any chance" and makes a question to a stranger softer. **씨** after a name is polite between adults on an equal footing, but not for someone clearly older or senior, and never after your own name. 반가워요 you know from Everyday Korean phrases.

**Speech level:** Alex and Minji use 해요체, the polite style for adults who have just met. The staff's lines sound different; the next step shows why."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-cafe-1a> ,
                         <#q-cafe-1b> .

<#q-cafe-1a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-16T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "저도 방금 왔어요"@ko ;
    solid-memo:back "I just got here too"@en ,
                    "Jag har också precis kommit"@sv ,
                    "Ich bin auch gerade gekommen"@de ,
                    "Yo también acabo de llegar"@es ,
                    "Moi aussi, je viens d'arriver"@fr ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Said to someone you are meeting who asks 많이 기다렸어요?, in polite 해요체. It is the usual answer even when it is not quite true; 방금 means just now and 저도 means me too."@en ,
                        "Sägs till någon man ska träffa som frågar 많이 기다렸어요?, i artig 해요체. Det är det vanliga svaret även när det inte riktigt stämmer; 방금 betyder nyss och 저도 betyder jag också."@sv ,
                        "Sagt man zu jemandem, mit dem man verabredet ist und der 많이 기다렸어요? fragt, im höflichen 해요체. Es ist die übliche Antwort, auch wenn sie nicht ganz stimmt; 방금 heißt gerade eben, 저도 heißt ich auch."@de ,
                        "Se le dice a la persona con la que has quedado cuando pregunta 많이 기다렸어요?, en el estilo cortés 해요체. Es la respuesta habitual aunque no sea del todo cierto; 방금 significa hace un momento y 저도, yo también."@es ,
                        "Se dit à la personne qu'on retrouve quand elle demande 많이 기다렸어요?, au style poli 해요체. C'est la réponse habituelle même si ce n'est pas tout à fait vrai ; 방금 veut dire à l'instant et 저도, moi aussi."@fr ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-cafe-1a-d1> ,
                          <#q-cafe-1a-d2> ,
                          <#q-cafe-1a-d3> .

