<#ch-cafe-2>
    a solid-memo:Step ,
      schema:LearningResource ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 1 ;
    schema:isPartOf <#ch-cafe> ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    solid-memo:textFormat solid-memo:markdown ;
    solid-memo:theory """## Choosing and counting

*The order could just as well have gone like this, with Alex ordering for both of them.*

**알렉스:** 아메리카노 두 잔 주세요.\\
*Two americanos, please.*

**직원:** 따뜻한 걸로 드릴까요?\\
*Would you like them hot?*

**알렉스:** 아니요, 아이스로 주세요.\\
*No, iced, please.*

**(으)로 for choosing.** Put 로 after the option you pick, or 으로 after a consonant other than ㄹ: 아이스로 (iced), 작은 사이즈로 (in a small size), 두유로 (with soy milk). 따뜻한 걸로 is short for 따뜻한 것으로, "the hot one". 이걸로 할게요, which you know from Everyday Korean phrases, works the same way.

**잔 with native numbers.** 잔 counts cups and glasses. It takes the native Korean numbers, and the first four get shorter before it: 한 잔, 두 잔, 세 잔, 네 잔, then 다섯 잔. The order is always drink, number, counter word, 주세요.

---

**Hearing -시-.** Staff speak to customers with -시-, which honors the person doing the action, here the customer: 주문하시겠어요? (are you ready to order?), 드시고 가세요? (for here?), 가지러 오세요 (please come and get it). 드시다 is the honorific word for eating and drinking, so 드시고 가세요? literally asks "will you have it and then go?"

You never use -시- about yourself, so you answer with the plain verb: 네, 마시고 갈게요 (yes, we'll have it here) or 아니요, 가져갈게요 (no, I'll take it with me).

**드릴까요?** works the other way round: 드리다 is the humble word for give, so the staff lower themselves instead of raising you. You answer with 주세요."""@en ;
    solid-memo:checkedBy <#q-cafe-2a> ,
                         <#q-cafe-2b> ,
                         <#q-cafe-2c> .

<#q-cafe-2a>
    a solid-memo:Card ;
    solid-memo:formatVersion 5 ;
    dcterms:created "2026-10-16T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    solid-memo:front "아이스로 주세요"@ko ;
    solid-memo:back "Iced, please"@en ,
                    "Med is, tack"@sv ,
                    "Mit Eis, bitte"@de ,
                    "Con hielo, por favor"@es ,
                    "Glacé, s'il vous plaît"@fr ;
    solid-memo:backNote "Said by a customer to café staff who ask whether it should be hot or iced, in polite 해요체. (으)로 marks the option you choose; for a hot drink you say 따뜻한 걸로 주세요."@en ,
                        "Sägs av en kund till personalen på ett kafé som frågar om det ska vara varmt eller med is, i artig 해요체. (으)로 markerar det alternativ man väljer; för en varm dryck säger man 따뜻한 걸로 주세요."@sv ,
                        "Sagt ein Gast zum Café-Personal, das fragt, ob es heiß oder mit Eis sein soll, im höflichen 해요체. (으)로 markiert die gewählte Möglichkeit; für ein heißes Getränk sagt man 따뜻한 걸로 주세요."@de ,
                        "Lo dice un cliente al personal de una cafetería que pregunta si lo quiere caliente o con hielo, en el estilo cortés 해요체. (으)로 marca la opción que eliges; para una bebida caliente se dice 따뜻한 걸로 주세요."@es ,
                        "Dit par un client au personnel d'un café qui demande s'il le veut chaud ou glacé, au style poli 해요체. (으)로 marque l'option choisie ; pour une boisson chaude, on dit 따뜻한 걸로 주세요."@fr ;
    solid-memo:distractor <#q-cafe-2a-d1> ,
                          <#q-cafe-2a-d2> ,
                          <#q-cafe-2a-d3> .

